    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In the theory of learning, the rate at which a subject is
                memorized is assumed to be proportional to the amount of
                material that is left to be memorized.
                Let <m>M</m> be the total amount of material that needs to be
                memorized, and note that <m>M</m> is a constant.
                Let <m>A(t)</m> be the amount of material that is memorized at
                time <m>t \geq 0</m>.
                Assume that no material is memorized at time 0.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Identify the independent and dependent variable(s), and any
                    parameters.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a differential equation for this scenario and involve
                    a schematic in your answer.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Assume that material is forgotten at a rate that is
                    proportional to the amount memorized at time
                    <m>t \geq 0</m>.
                    Expand on the model in Part (b) to include forgetfulness.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does it mean about your differential equation if you
                    are forgetting material quicker than you are memorizing
                    material?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>